  "wardrobeComment": "Every piece a soldier may be dressed in at the muster ground's dress board (the author, 29 October 2026: a troop editor, many clothes, within a culture, fair per stage of development): its layers, its slot, the rank (level) it opens at, who may wear it ('common' for spearman, archer, crossbowman; 'knight' for the man-at-arms, who may also wear common pieces) and the cultures it belongs to ('all', or english / french / german: the Hundsgugel German, the great bascinet western). Cosmetic: nothing here changes a fight. The years are for the historiker and never shown. Garment, padding, mail, plates, surcoat, hood, helmet and bevor may each also be left off. Weapons are chosen too, but only among those a kind of soldier is trained for ('units'): a spearman may carry a sword, an archer only his bow. The weapon slot is never empty. The longbow is English (the historiker).",
  "bodiesComment": "An era's body armour, where it has one, takes the place of the outfit's padding, mail, plates and surcoat (historiker's pre-check, 31 October 2026). Man-at-arms: from 1360 plate arms and legs under a short padded jupon (the Black Prince's effigy, 1376); from 1400 in England and France the open white harness (Capwell 2015), in the Empire the jupon a while longer (filled in, thin for the Leine); from 1440 the white harness everywhere (the Beauchamp effigy). Common men: from 1400 the quilted jack in England and France; in the Empire mail over the padded coat (filled in, thin); from 1440 the jack in England (Bridport muster roll 1457: 67 jacks, 2 brigandines) and France (the ordinance of Montils-les-Tours 1448: 'jaque ou brigandine'), the brigandine in the Empire (filled in, after south German pictures and the Wolfegg Housebook). The English archer of 1400-1440 often wore none (Monstrelet, Waurin). No jack of plate (16th century).",
  "weaponsComment": "An era's weapon in place of the outfit's own (historiker's post-check, 31 October 2026): the man-at-arms in white harness fights on foot with a pollaxe, no shield (Azincourt 1415; the Beauchamp Pageant, c. 1485; Capwell 2015), from 1400 in England and France, from 1440 in the Empire, where the jupon and its shield last longer; the English foot soldier from 1400 carries the bill (Bridport 1457; the commissions of array)."
}
